<section aria-labelledby="what-title"><h2 id="what-title">What is it?</h2>
<p>Bonfire is open-source software for running your own community. Readers get profiles, conversations, groups and topics. Your team decides who can join each space and who moderates it.</p>
<p>For publishers, we connect that community to the work you already do. Articles become places to talk. Memberships can open access to groups and content. Your design, everywhere, not ours.</p>
<p>You can install, configure and customise Bonfire yourself. There is no software licence fee and no requirement to buy our services. <a href="/self-hosting/">Start with the self-hosting guide</a>.</p></section>
<section aria-labelledby="jacobin-title"><h2 id="jacobin-title">Built with publishers, for publishers</h2>
<p>Jacobin's readers asked for a protected place to discuss, bring story ideas to the newsroom and see behind the scenes. They already felt part of a community and wanted a space that reflected it.</p>
<p>We connected their website and Bonfire, imported their archive, mapped subscriptions to access permissions and created a custom interface. Journalists and editors take part directly; Bonfire handles technical maintenance and support.</p>
<p><a href="https://jacobin.de/social/">Read why Jacobin built it</a>, or <a href="/case-studies/jacobin/">explore the full case study</a>.</p></section>
